<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chen Jiongming</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chen Jiongming was a Chinese military leader and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>. He brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> in 1920 and backed his government, but he favoured a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and opposed Sun's plans for a northern campaign. In June 1922 his troops shelled Sun's residence and drove him out; he was finally defeated by the Whampoa-trained forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chen-jiongming.jpg" alt="Chen Jiongming" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chen_Jiongming2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chen was born on 18 January 1878 in Haifeng, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, to a landlord family of moderate means. He took the name Jiongming on gaining the xiucai degree in 1899, and in 1908 graduated at the top of his class from the Academy of Law and Political Science in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>. Zou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a>'s The Revolutionary Army, with its call for a republic on the American model, left him with a lifelong ideal of a federated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>Elected to the Guangdong Provincial Assembly in 1909, he led the campaign that ended legal gambling in 1911. He likely joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a> around 1909, sponsored the anarchist-inspired Chinese Assassination Corps, and in November 1911 led the Xun Army's rising at Huizhou, after which Guangdong declared independence and he became vice military governor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1918 to 1920 Chen governed twenty-six counties of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> from Zhangzhou, where his reforms earned it the name of a Little Model China. In 1920 he declined arms offered by an envoy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>. After his Guangdong Army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>, he became civil governor: he banned gambling and opium, made Chen Duxiu head of a provincial education committee, founded the University of Guangdong, and issued a charter for Canton in December 1920 and a provincial constitution in 1921.</p><p>He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>'s election as President-Extraordinary in April 1921 and his expedition north to reunify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by force. Sun dismissed him in April 1922. At 3:00 am on 16 June 1922 a detachment of the Guangdong Army moved against Sun's Presidential Palace; Sun had escaped to the warship Yongfeng, and the next day he had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> shelled from it, killing over a hundred civilians. Most scholars conclude that Chen did not plan the coup. In February 1925 Whampoa forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> began the First East River Campaign against his army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From Hong Kong Chen led the China Zhi Gong Party, formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> in 1925, and in 1927 published A Proposal for the Unification of China. He died poor in Hong Kong on 22 September 1933 and was buried on Ziwei Hill in Huizhou in 1935. Both Nationalist and Communist historians long portrayed him as a counter-revolutionary warlord, but since the late 20th century many scholars have reassessed him as a democrat and federalist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chen Jiongming” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Chen Jiongming</b> brought Sun back in 1920, and in 1921 he became extraordinary president, but Chen turned against him in 1922.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-military-government">Canton Military Government</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Haifeng County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.97,115.33&amp;year=1913" aria-label="Haifeng County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7632 1316 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chen Jiongming"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8082 1586h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8082 1586h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.97,115.33&amp;year=1913">What was here in 1913? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
